Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Throw past the exhaustive DatePart specifier switches

The two DatePartSpecifier dispatchers added in #24573 enumerate every
specifier and end there, so GCC reports -Wreturn-type on both: an enum
value outside the enumerators falls off the end of a non-void function.
The templated helpers just above them do not, because their default
label makes the switch exhaustive to the compiler.

Restating the switch's own NotImplementedException after it is the
spelling the rest of the tree uses for that shape, and it keeps -Wswitch
reporting a specifier a later change forgets to handle.
---
diff --git a/src/duckdb/extension/core_functions/scalar/date/date_part.cpp b/src/duckdb/extension/core_functions/scalar/date/date_part.cpp
index 1b9808481..9d768b473 100644
--- a/src/duckdb/extension/core_functions/scalar/date/date_part.cpp
+++ b/src/duckdb/extension/core_functions/scalar/date/date_part.cpp
@@ -1932,6 +1932,7 @@ static scalar_function_t DatePartUnaryCallback(DatePartSpecifier part_code, Logi
 	case DatePartSpecifier::INVALID:
 		throw NotImplementedException("Specifier type not implemented for DATEPART");
 	}
+	throw NotImplementedException("Specifier type not implemented for DATEPART");
 }
 
 template <typename OP>
@@ -2011,6 +2012,7 @@ static function_statistics_t DatePartUnaryStatistics(DatePartSpecifier part_code
 	case DatePartSpecifier::INVALID:
 		throw NotImplementedException("Specifier type not implemented for DATEPART");
 	}
+	throw NotImplementedException("Specifier type not implemented for DATEPART");
 }
 
 unique_ptr<FunctionData> DatePartBind(BindScalarFunctionInput &input) {
